<interrogationes unitas="arbor2_expandere">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I macro-expand a C source buffer into tokens with provenance?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_expansion_creare arbor2_expansion_processare Arbor2Token">Create
      a context with arbor2_expansion_creare(piscina, intern), add any
      predefined macros, then call arbor2_expansion_processare(exp,
      fons, mensura, via_file). It lexes the buffer into layer 0 and
      expands layer by layer until nothing changes (at most C = 100
      layers), returning the last layer as a Xar of Arbor2Token*
      (ending with the EOF token - or with an ERROR token: lexing stops
      at the first one, so the rest of the buffer is dropped). Each
      token carries where it came from (source or which macro, at which
      layer).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I make Latin house code (si, redde, nomen) expand to plain C keywords?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_includere_latina arbor2_expansion_addere_macro">Call
      arbor2_includere_latina(exp) before processing. It registers a
      fixed table of the keyword, type, qualifier and storage macros
      (si -&gt; if, nomen -&gt; typedef, interior -&gt; static, NIHIL
      -&gt; NULL, VERUM 1, FALSUM 0). Despite its comment it does NOT
      register everything in latina.h: ZEPHYRUM, the Roman numerals
      (I .. MMMCMXCIX), principale, imprimere, liberare,
      memoriae_allocare, exire and FILUM are missing, so add those with
      arbor2_expansion_addere_macro if you need them expanded.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I predefine object-like and function-like macros from code?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_expansion_addere_macro arbor2_expansion_addere_macro_functio">arbor2_expansion_addere_macro(exp,
      name, value, via_file) lexes the value string as the body;
      arbor2_expansion_addere_macro_functio takes a NIHIL-terminated
      array of parameter names (an entry "..." makes it variadic) and a
      body string. via_file may be NIHIL. Both mark the definition
      ex_api with linea_def -1, and replace any macro of the same
      name.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can it handle #if, #ifdef and #ifndef include guards?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_expansion_processare">No.
      The expander acts only on #define, #undef and #include (a # that
      is the first token or has whitespace before it, followed by one
      of those words; the check does not require a newline). Every other
      directive, including #if, #ifdef, #ifndef, #else and #endif,
      passes through as ordinary tokens and both branches are
      expanded, so a #define inside a false branch still takes
      effect.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I make #include find and expand header files?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_expansion_addere_local_via arbor2_expansion_addere_system_via arbor2_expansion_resolvere_include">Add
      search directories with arbor2_expansion_addere_local_via (for
      "...") and arbor2_expansion_addere_system_via (for &lt;...&gt;).
      A quoted include is tried next to the current file first, then in
      the local paths, then the system paths; an angle include only in
      the system paths. An unresolved include is skipped silently and
      its directive tokens are dropped from the output. The
      genus constants ARBOR2_INCLUDE_LOCAL and ARBOR2_INCLUDE_SYSTEM
      live in arbor2_glr.h, not in this header. Caution (audit
      2026-10-18, unfixed): in the default ARBOR2_INCLUDE_RESET_PER_PARSE
      mode the nested processare call wipes the already-included mark,
      so a header included twice is expanded twice and a header that
      includes itself (directly or through another) recurses until the
      stack overflows; set ARBOR2_INCLUDE_PERSISTENT first.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the difference between learning mode and full mode for includes?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_expansion_processare Arbor2Expansion">Both
      read and expand the included file, so its macros and typedefs
      enter the shared tables. In full mode its tokens are also spliced
      into the result in place of the directive; in learning mode they
      are thrown away. By default system (&lt;&gt;) includes are
      learning (system_learning_default VERUM) and local ("") includes
      are full; change the two fields on the context to switch.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can the default include mode skip a header that was already included?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_expansion_ponere_include_modum arbor2_expansion_processare">Not
      reliably. ARBOR2_INCLUDE_RESET_PER_PARSE (the default) replaces
      the included-files table at the start of EVERY
      arbor2_expansion_processare call, and an include runs processare
      recursively on the header, which wipes the mark it just set. So
      the same header included twice is processed twice, and a header
      that includes itself (directly or through another) recurses
      without end. Set ARBOR2_INCLUDE_PERSISTENT with
      arbor2_expansion_ponere_include_modum to get skipping; the table
      then also survives across processare calls, so a header already
      read for an earlier file is skipped for the next one.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a self-referential macro like define F (1+F) expand correctly?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_expansion_processare arbor2_expansion_obtinere_layers">No.
      The recursion guard (macro_stack) only holds a name while that
      one macro's body is being copied; the copied F is expanded again
      on the NEXT layer, where the stack is empty. Expansion stops only
      at the cap of C (100) layers, silently, so F ends up nested about
      100 deep instead of C's (1+F). A plain define SELF SELF ends as
      SELF, but only after running all 100 layers.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I ask which macros were defined at a given source line?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_expansion_macros_ad_lineam arbor2_expansion_obtinere_segmenta Arbor2Segmentum">Not
      really. Segments are opened at each #define and #undef, but every
      segment stores a pointer to the one live macro table, not a copy
      (the code says so: true isolation would need a copy function).
      arbor2_expansion_macros_ad_lineam therefore returns the table as
      it stands after processing, whatever line you ask for. Segment
      line numbers also do not say which file they belong to.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I check whether an identifier is a typedef name?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_expansion_est_typedef arbor2_expansion_quaerere_typedef arbor2_expansion_addere_typedef Arbor2TypedefInfo">arbor2_expansion_est_typedef(exp,
      name) after processing; arbor2_expansion_quaerere_typedef returns
      an Arbor2TypedefInfo with the layer and source position. Typedefs
      are detected on every layer from a typedef keyword (est_certum
      VERUM) or an unexpanded nomen identifier (heuristic, est_certum
      FALSUM): the name is the (*NAME) of a function pointer, else the
      last identifier at depth 0 before the semicolon. The first
      detection wins (a detected name never overwrites an existing
      entry), so a nomen typedef keeps est_certum FALSUM even after nomen
      expands to typedef on a later layer. Add names by hand with
      arbor2_expansion_addere_typedef (layer -1, est_certum
      VERUM).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does arbor2_expansion_lookahead tell the GLR parser?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_expansion_lookahead arbor2_expansion_lookahead_genus Arbor2ExpansionLookahead" via="lib/arbor2_glr.c">The
      genus of the first token of a macro's body without expanding it:
      ARBOR2_LEXEMA_ERROR if the name is not a macro, ARBOR2_LEXEMA_EOF
      (est_vacuum VERUM) for an empty body. est_recursivum is VERUM when
      that first token is itself a macro name, so the caller must look
      further.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I reuse one Arbor2Expansion for many files?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_expansion_processare arbor2_expansion_obtinere_layers arbor2_expansion_obtinere_segmenta">Yes,
      and macros and typedefs carry over between calls (that is how a
      header's definitions reach the next file). But the layers and
      segments are never reset: those of every call, including nested
      include calls, accumulate in the same Xars, and everything lives
      in the context's piscina until you drop it.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
